  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Tied through six innings until Gelof made it matter in the seventh.",
    "what_this_means": "They left six runners on base. Three in the critical third inning when the game was still even. That's the pattern holding. Lopez kept them in it for six innings despite the inefficiency, and the bullpen (Medina, Alvarado, Rom, Harris) ran four clean arms for 2.3 scoreless to give the bats a chance. Butler drove in two runs early. Gelof broke the tie in the seventh with the go-ahead hit. This is what the offense looks like when it stops waiting. Runners move, pressure arrives late, and one clutch piece finishes what the lineup started. The formula is still fragile because it depends on someone delivering in that exact moment, but it delivered today. Eight left on base. Two mattered.",
    "what_to_watch": "Kansas City sends Michael Wacha (3.46 ERA) tonight at 4:40 PT. The Athletics need to do something the lineup hasn't sustained yet. String together clean at-bats instead of loading bases and hoping.",
